[[:db/retract 17592186124813 :question/answer-fields 17592186121475] {:db/id 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-da2e1b4b-fd99-52e9-91fa-fb0d43968219"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-da2e1b4b-fd99-52e9-91fa-fb0d43968219" :question/id #uuid "da2e1b4b-fd99-52e9-91fa-fb0d43968219" :question/math-academy-id "q-164157" :question/problem "![](images/19/19f4f2d616fa648fcd4663be22b32ab18662fffa8e31f9bd44f0dd3b6da99208.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch region exists for the point $A.$ Hence, $A$ is a local maximum point.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b16c2cb372280ec39915b0e8d4e396e68baec9a8e20edc5389ced079648836.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-261ceadd-9e63-5d86-9762-c5e81f0679da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-261ceadd-9e63-5d86-9762-c5e81f0679da" :answer-field/id #uuid "261ceadd-9e63-5d86-9762-c5e81f0679da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e2b33820-f35d-5434-abf3-958f348e4695" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e2b33820-f35d-5434-abf3-958f348e4695" "answer-80185868-d8d6-5a9a-9b8a-83d741afd936" "answer-b9b3e9e9-2073-5961-8289-000100206e82" "answer-b251a48a-e4be-5932-a217-f573cfba9f2d" "answer-118339a7-669